1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,500
Imagine a massive earthquake
hits a city.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,000
The shaking stops,
but the danger doesn't.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,500
Aftershocks can continue
for days, weeks, even months,

4
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:13,500
and scientists need to know
exactly where they're happening.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,500
They track them with seismometers:
instruments that measure ground vibrations.

6
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:22,000
After a big quake, teams rush a few
portable seismometers into the field.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
But they can't just install more.

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Every sensor costs money and time,
some sites are hard to reach,

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
and sensors placed close together
record the same information twice.

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The real challenge
isn't more sensors.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,500
It's the right locations.

12
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,500
That's why we built QuakeNet-Q, for the
Disaster Response domain of Qiskit Fall Fest 2026:

13
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,000
an automated agent that uses quantum optimisation
to decide where portable seismometers go.

14
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:50,000
Here's how it works.
The agent watches the USGS earthquake feed.

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,500
When a large quake hits,
it starts by itself.

16
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:56,500
It takes real aftershock data
and the existing EarthScope stations,

17
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:01,000
and scores every possible network
with D-optimality,

18
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,500
a measure of how much location information
a network gives.

19
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Then comes the quantum part.

20
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:11,500
In our prototype we choose 4 sensors
from 10 sites: 210 combinations.

21
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,500
In Qiskit, each site is one qubit.

22
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,000
A Dicke state and an XY mixer keep the QAOA circuit
on networks of exactly four sensors,

23
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
and we encode the score exactly,
not approximately.

24
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
A classical optimiser tunes the circuit
so the best networks become more likely.

25
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Every answer is checked
against an exact search.

26
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,500
Then, every hour, the agent checks the new aftershocks
and re-plans if the activity has moved.

27
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Here it is live:
the trigger setting, a plan,

28
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
and Ridgecrest replayed
hour by hour.

29
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,500
On the 2019 Ridgecrest earthquake, the quantum step
found the best network in 15 of 15 runs.

30
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:19,000
One measurement picks the best network up to 11%
of the time, against 0.48% for random guessing.

31
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
In a simulated test on later aftershocks, our network
cut location error from 3 kilometres to 2.3,

32
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,500
better than a standard field rule.

33
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:29,500
And a full 24-hour replay
runs in about 20 seconds.

34
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,500
We're honest about the limits.

35
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:37,000
At this size a classical computer is just as fast,
so we claim no quantum speedup.

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
And on four of five other earthquakes,
the simple field rule did as well or better.

37
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
That tells us the next step is a better score,
not a faster search.

38
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:50,000
QuakeNet-Q: the right sensors, in the right places,
within minutes of the quake.
